    {
      "id": "death-valley",
      "name": "Death Valley",
      "state": "CA/NV",
      "lat": 36.5054,
      "lng": -117.0794,
      "visited": true,
      "visitDates": "Mar 17-19, 2023",
      "blurb": "Me and my dear friend Anna went to Death Valley for a couple of days while she was visiting me in Santa Barbara. Baker was maybe the unexpected highlight of the road trip, since we are both lifelong fans of aliens and alien-themed kitsch. I somehow managed to be absurdly underprepared for the desert, though — I completely forgot to bring sunscreen (it cost like $15 at the Death Valley visitor center!) and my sleeping bag was absolutely not warm enough for the cold desert nights. Fortunately Anna was, as always, wonderfully forgiving of my mishaps. We had an amazing time driving around the park and getting out for some short hikes in the heat. My favorite part was Badwater Basin — talk about an alien landscape.",
      "photo": "death-valley.jpg"
    },
    {
      "id": "denali",
      "name": "Denali",
      "state": "AK",
      "lat": 63.1148,
      "lng": -151.1926,
      "visited": true,
      "visitDates": "Aug 30-Sep 3, 2022",
      "blurb": "My buddy Jake planned the route for our backcountry loop through Denali National Park, since he had been backpacking before and I had not. He was very worried about running into a bear, but I felt like the chances were low that we'd get to see a wild bear from afar, let alone bump right into one... Well, what do you know — not even an hour after getting off the bus and starting the hike along the riverbed to our first campsite, we see two bears directly in our path, pawing at the river. They were pretty far out and seemingly hadn't spotted us, so we scrambled up a hill through the brush to find a long way around them — and at the top of that hill was a HUGE moose, staring us down. Fun fact: Moose are one of the only animals in the Denali wilderness that you're actually *supposed* to run from, since they aren't predators — so we ran through the brush off in a different direction, only to come across two juvenile moose who seemed as perturbed by us as we were by them... So we changed course yet again. After maybe an hour, we were way off track, but at least we knew we were far enough downriver to have gotten past the bears. Jake started redirecting us back onto our planned route. We finally made it back to the riverbed, at least a mile away from where we started, only to find those two same bears blocking our route again. It turned out they had been moseying this way the whole time we were scrambling through the brush.Jacob Jonathan Jackson, you might be the only person on this earth who could talk me into doing something as crazy as this, but I would do it again in a heartbeat!",
      "photo": "denali.jpg"
    },
